1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,050
The Ghee Hin and the 1890
Ban on the Secret Societies

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:08,887
On the 1st of January 1890,
a new law came into force

3
00:00:08,967 --> 00:00:12,582
in the Straits Settlements,
and with it the oldest

4
00:00:12,662 --> 00:00:16,875
Chinese society in Singapore,
the Ghee Hin, had to close.

5
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:20,858
For more than sixty years the
secret societies had taken

6
00:00:20,938 --> 00:00:24,049
in new arrivals from
China, found them work,

7
00:00:24,129 --> 00:00:28,402
settled their quarrels and
fought each other in the streets,

8
00:00:28,482 --> 00:00:33,125
and by 1888 they counted some
65,000 members in Singapore alone.

9
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:37,495
Within months, the headmen of
the ten societies the government

10
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:42,025
had declared dangerous handed
in their seals, flags and books.

11
00:00:42,025 --> 00:00:47,414
The societies grew out of the
Tiandihui, the Heaven and Earth Society,

12
00:00:47,494 --> 00:00:52,025
a sworn brotherhood that began
in Fujian in the mid-1700s.

13
00:00:52,025 --> 00:00:56,716
Its members swore to overthrow the
Qing dynasty and restore the Ming,

14
00:00:56,796 --> 00:00:59,967
and as Chinese migrants
spread across Southeast

15
00:01:00,047 --> 00:01:03,850
Asia they took its lodges,
oaths and rituals with them.

16
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:07,321
New members were initiated
in an elaborate ceremony

17
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:11,289
and learned secret signs,
passwords and ways of arranging

18
00:01:11,369 --> 00:01:14,850
teacups by which they could
recognise one another.

19
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:19,099
The Ghee Hin, whose name means
"the rise of righteousness,"

20
00:01:19,179 --> 00:01:22,700
is regarded as Singapore's
first secret society.

21
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:26,381
It was set up in the 1820s,
soon after the British

22
00:01:26,461 --> 00:01:31,050
settlement was founded, with
its headquarters on Rochor Road.

23
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:33,675
At first it was a mutual aid society,

24
00:01:33,755 --> 00:01:37,850
and it even helped the police
by turning in lawbreakers.

25
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:42,187
Rival societies formed in the
1840s, among them the Ghee Hok,

26
00:01:42,267 --> 00:01:44,722
started by former Ghee Hin members,

27
00:01:44,802 --> 00:01:49,067
and others split along the lines
of the main dialect groups,

28
00:01:49,147 --> 00:01:53,275
the Hokkiens, the Teochews,
the Cantonese and the Hakkas.

29
00:01:53,275 --> 00:01:57,000
Singapore's Chinese population grew fast.

30
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,195
In 1824 there were about
3,300 Chinese in a town

31
00:02:02,275 --> 00:02:07,250
of about 10,700 people,
and by 1860 there were

32
00:02:07,330 --> 00:02:12,275
about 50,000 in a
population of about 82,000.

33
00:02:12,275 --> 00:02:16,625
Many arrived alone, with
no family here to turn to.

34
00:02:16,625 --> 00:02:20,600
The societies met them off the
ships, gave them a brotherhood,

35
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:24,525
lent them money, found them
work and settled their disputes,

36
00:02:24,605 --> 00:02:27,875
and in return they took
dues and demanded loyalty.

37
00:02:27,875 --> 00:02:32,150
The colonial government had
few ways of reaching these men.

38
00:02:32,150 --> 00:02:38,076
Until 1871 it had no European
officer who spoke Chinese well,

39
00:02:38,156 --> 00:02:41,030
and its police force was tiny:

40
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:46,525
in 1831 it had 18 men for a
population of about 16,000.

41
00:02:46,525 --> 00:02:50,336
The societies filled the
gap, and from the 1830s they

42
00:02:50,416 --> 00:02:54,448
also controlled much of the
pepper and gambier planting,

43
00:02:54,528 --> 00:02:58,125
the rice trade, gambling,
opium and prostitution.

44
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:00,454
As I wrote in the post on opium,

45
00:03:00,534 --> 00:03:05,125
the colony's own revenue depended
on some of the same trades.

46
00:03:05,125 --> 00:03:09,650
The rivalry between the
societies often turned violent.

47
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:14,044
In March 1846, when the head of
the Ghee Hin, Ho Ah Yam, died,

48
00:03:14,124 --> 00:03:18,735
about 6,000 members marched in
his funeral procession, and two to

49
00:03:18,815 --> 00:03:23,650
three thousand armed men of the
Ghee Hok gathered to confront them.

50
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:27,950
The police, backed by troops,
kept the two sides apart.

51
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:33,114
In February 1851, Ghee Hin men
attacked the plantations of Chinese

52
00:03:33,194 --> 00:03:38,200
Christians for about a week, and
nearly 500 people were killed.

53
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:43,177
The worst riots came in May 1854,
between Hokkiens and Teochews,

54
00:03:43,257 --> 00:03:46,575
and between the Ghee Hin and the Ghee Hok.

55
00:03:46,575 --> 00:03:49,720
They began on the 5th
of May with a quarrel

56
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,800
over the price of a few catties of rice.

57
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:56,408
The chronicler Charles Burton
Buckley wrote that the real

58
00:03:56,488 --> 00:03:59,967
cause was the Hokkiens'
refusal to contribute to a fund

59
00:04:00,047 --> 00:04:03,396
for rebels who had been
driven out of Amoy, in China,

60
00:04:03,476 --> 00:04:07,100
and that the fighting had
been planned in the societies.

61
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:12,091
For ten or twelve days it spread from
the town to the country districts,

62
00:04:12,171 --> 00:04:14,556
from Rochor to Paya Lebar, Geylang,

63
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:18,650
Siglap, Tanglin and the roads
to Bukit Timah and Thomson.

64
00:04:18,650 --> 00:04:24,100
Buckley counted more than 400
dead and about 300 houses burnt.

65
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:26,655
In June a special criminal session

66
00:04:26,735 --> 00:04:29,525
of the court tried the men arrested.

67
00:04:29,525 --> 00:04:32,725
Smaller outbreaks followed for decades.

68
00:04:32,725 --> 00:04:36,289
In December 1876, Teochew
merchants opposed to a new

69
00:04:36,369 --> 00:04:41,053
government sub-post office for
letters and money sent home to China,

70
00:04:41,133 --> 00:04:43,576
which threatened their own business,

71
00:04:43,656 --> 00:04:48,000
stirred up a riot on the day it
opened, and three people died.

72
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,157
The colony's first answer was to register

73
00:04:51,237 --> 00:04:54,000
the societies rather than ban them.

74
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,770
The Dangerous Societies
Suppression Ordinance of 1869

75
00:04:57,850 --> 00:05:02,565
required them to register their
names, office-bearers and members,

76
00:05:02,645 --> 00:05:06,350
so that the government at
least knew who they were.

77
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:10,075
In practice, the registers
were easily faked.

78
00:05:10,075 --> 00:05:13,718
Rules and membership lists
were made up, and the men

79
00:05:13,798 --> 00:05:17,950
named as office-bearers were
often not the ones in charge.

80
00:05:17,950 --> 00:05:22,027
In 1877 the government set
up the Chinese Protectorate

81
00:05:22,107 --> 00:05:26,877
under William Pickering, who
became Registrar of Societies too,

82
00:05:26,957 --> 00:05:30,575
as I described in the
post on the Protectorate.

83
00:05:30,575 --> 00:05:33,909
Pickering preferred to
watch the societies and work

84
00:05:33,989 --> 00:05:37,123
through their headmen,
and the Protectorate gave

85
00:05:37,203 --> 00:05:40,550
Chinese residents a place
to bring their disputes.

86
00:05:40,550 --> 00:05:45,239
Further laws in 1882 and
1885 tightened the rules,

87
00:05:45,319 --> 00:05:48,275
but the societies kept growing.

88
00:05:48,275 --> 00:05:52,768
In Singapore their membership rose from

89
00:05:52,848 --> 00:05:58,125
about 49,000 in 1885 to
about 65,000 in 1888.

90
00:05:58,125 --> 00:06:01,828
In July 1887 a man put
up to it by members of

91
00:06:01,908 --> 00:06:05,775
the Ghee Hok attacked
Pickering in his office.

92
00:06:05,775 --> 00:06:08,933
A few months later, in October 1887,

93
00:06:09,013 --> 00:06:12,700
Sir Cecil Clementi Smith became Governor.

94
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:17,263
He had served in Hong Kong for
sixteen years, and he decided that

95
00:06:17,343 --> 00:06:21,700
registration had failed and the
societies must be suppressed.

96
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:26,050
Both Pickering and the head of
the police opposed his bill.

97
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,199
When it came before the Legislative
Council in February 1889,

98
00:06:30,279 --> 00:06:34,151
the unofficial members, who
were not government officers,

99
00:06:34,231 --> 00:06:37,975
voted against it, but the
officials' votes carried it.

100
00:06:37,975 --> 00:06:42,612
The Societies Ordinance of
1889 came into force on the 1st

101
00:06:42,692 --> 00:06:47,247
of January 1890, with six
months' grace for the societies

102
00:06:47,327 --> 00:06:51,800
to wind up their affairs and
dispose of their property.

103
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:56,219
Under it, any association of ten
or more people was a society,

104
00:06:56,299 --> 00:06:59,701
every society had to be
registered to be lawful,

105
00:06:59,781 --> 00:07:04,425
and the government could dissolve
the existing secret societies.

106
00:07:04,425 --> 00:07:07,741
In March 1890 the
Governor reported that in

107
00:07:07,821 --> 00:07:11,849
Singapore the headmen of
all ten dangerous societies

108
00:07:11,929 --> 00:07:15,325
had handed in their
seals, flags and books.

109
00:07:15,325 --> 00:07:18,267
Most of the Ghee Hin's
papers were destroyed,

110
00:07:18,347 --> 00:07:22,027
but a few seals and certificates
went to the Protector's

111
00:07:22,107 --> 00:07:25,800
office and are now in the
National Museum of Singapore.

112
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:28,694
To keep a link with the Chinese community,

113
00:07:28,774 --> 00:07:33,297
the government set up a Chinese
Advisory Board in each settlement

114
00:07:33,377 --> 00:07:37,404
in December 1889, with members
drawn from the main dialect

115
00:07:37,484 --> 00:07:41,025
groups and the Protector
of Chinese sitting on it.

116
00:07:41,025 --> 00:07:46,425
The boards advised the government on
Chinese affairs and arbitrated disputes,

117
00:07:46,505 --> 00:07:49,850
work that the societies'
headmen had once done.

118
00:07:49,850 --> 00:07:52,950
The ban did not end the societies.

119
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:56,383
Some went underground,
and others registered as

120
00:07:56,463 --> 00:08:00,500
recreational or social clubs
and carried on as before.

121
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:04,299
The clan and dialect
associations, which ran temples,

122
00:08:04,379 --> 00:08:07,885
schools and burial grounds
for their communities,

123
00:08:07,965 --> 00:08:10,600
carried on openly under the new law.

124
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:14,516
M. L. Wynne, a police officer
who studied the societies,

125
00:08:14,596 --> 00:08:20,010
summed up the dilemma: "The inherent
weakness of toleration is encouragement;

126
00:08:20,090 --> 00:08:24,300
of registration, recognition;
and of suppression, evasion."

127
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:28,038
Gangs descended from the
old societies remained a

128
00:08:28,118 --> 00:08:32,090
problem for decades, until
tougher criminal laws and

129
00:08:32,170 --> 00:08:36,300
police operations in the
1950s broke up many of them.

130
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:38,974
The rule that began in 1890 is

131
00:08:39,054 --> 00:08:41,900
still in force in another form.

132
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:46,193
Under Singapore's Societies
Act, any club or association

133
00:08:46,273 --> 00:08:51,815
of ten or more people must be registered
with the Registry of Societies,

134
00:08:51,895 --> 00:08:55,175
the same threshold the 1889 ordinance set.

135
00:08:55,175 --> 00:08:59,503
Where it fits in the bigger story:
For most of the 19th century,

136
00:08:59,583 --> 00:09:05,220
colonial Singapore relied on the secret
societies to manage its Chinese population,

137
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:08,400
and then decided it
could not live with them.

138
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:12,125
The 1890 ban was a turning
point in how the state dealt

139
00:09:12,205 --> 00:09:15,445
with the Chinese community,
from working through

140
00:09:15,525 --> 00:09:20,011
brotherhoods it could not control
to registering every association

141
00:09:20,091 --> 00:09:23,550
and building its own links
with community leaders.

142
00:09:23,550 --> 00:09:28,625
The registration rule it introduced
has outlasted the colony.
